Getting into the BIOS
Reboot the NAS and tap Ctrl+F2 (or Ctrl+F12) right at power-on, or SSH into UGOS and run sudo systemctl reboot --firmware-setup to land there automatically next reboot.

Chipset → System Agent (SA) Configuration → PCI Express Configuration
This is where the individual PCIe root ports live (Root Port 1, 2, 3, plus the M.2 SSD1 slot). Go into each one and set ASPM to Disabled.

M.2 SSD1: confirmed already Disabled ✓
Root Port 1 / 2 / 3: still need to check each — you saw the list but hadn't opened them individually yet

Advanced → SATA Configuration

Aggressive LPM Support → set to Disabled (this was Enabled — the SATA-side equivalent of ASPM, and a common cause of drive stalls/dropouts on its own)
Leave SATA Mode Selection on AHCI and SATA Test Mode on Disabled — those are already correct

Still open / worth resolving

The BIOS watchdog timer — we flagged early on that it can force-reboot the system if UGOS isn't detected for a couple minutes, but we never located and disabled it in this session. Worth hunting down (often under Advanced, sometimes in a Super I/O or EC-related submenu) so it doesn't interrupt future BIOS visits.
Your SATA ports showed "No Install" for all three — if your actual drive bays were expected to show up there and didn't, they're likely on a separate controller with its own settings menu, which would need the same ASPM/LPM check independently.

Only if problems persist after the above

SA PCIe LTR Configuration → Force LTR Override → Enabled (forces the platform to ignore bad latency-tolerance signaling from a device)
PCI Express Clock Gating / PCI Express Power Gating on the M.2 SSD1 port → Disabled
CPU C-state limit under CPU Configuration / Power & Performance

Once you've hit the Root Ports and the watchdog, save with F10 and you should be in good shape.
